\description{
Produce WHEP's canonical spatial support unit, the \strong{polycell}: a
0.5-degree grid cell intersected with a polity over that polity's validity
interval. Each row carries the polity's territory in the cell decomposed into
three separately addressable categories,
\code{polity_area_ha = land_area_ha + inland_water_ha + ice_area_ha}, so that
aggregating polycells to a polity changes no absolute value and no quantity
crosses a border it does not belong to.

Areas are geodesic, from \code{sf::st_area()} on unprojected WGS84 with spherical
(\code{s2}) geometry, matching WHEP's own spherical convention; \code{cell_area_ha}
keeps the package formula so it stays bit-identical to
\code{\link[=build_cell_polity]{build_cell_polity()}}. Territory is the union of live real polities; land
claimed by no live polity is emitted in the \code{"unassigned"} attribute rather
than renormalised away.

The default grain is \strong{interval-keyed}: one row per polycell per interval
over which every area is constant, carrying \code{start_year} and \code{end_year}.
Supply \code{years} to expand to one row per polycell-year, which is what
\code{\link[=expand_polycell_years]{expand_polycell_years()}} does on demand. No area varies within an interval,
so the interval grain is the form to store.
}
\section{Land definitions in play}{

Four definitions of "land" are live in this pipeline and they disagree by
up to 10\%, so a global area only means something next to the definition it
was measured on. At 2015:\tabular{ll}{
   Definition \tab Global area \cr
   Whole 0.5-degree cells holding any land \tab 14.3195 Gha \cr
   HaNi's own land mask \tab 13.5977 Gha \cr
   Union of the live polity polygons \tab 13.4267 Gha \cr
   LUH2 terrestrial, \code{(1 - icwtr) * carea} \tab 12.9931 Gha \cr
}


\code{polity_area_ha} carries the third row's territory -- the polity polygons,
decomposed into land, inland water and ice -- but \strong{summing it does not
give the third row}. The union is unique ground, which is what makes it
comparable with the other three; a sum counts shared ground once per
claiming polity. At 2015 \code{sum(polity_area_ha)} is 13.4599 Gha, exceeding
the union by the 0.0332 Gha two live polities both claim. Quote the union
for a land definition and the sum for attributed territory, and never read
the difference between them as a leak. The first row is the
convention this table replaces -- a per-hectare rate multiplied by
\code{cell_area_ha} -- and it over-counts by 11.0\%. The fourth is the DA-5
validation layer: its disagreement with the polygons is emitted in the
\code{"unassigned"} attribute and never silently reconciled, and the polygons
exceeding it by about 2.2\% is what \code{inland_water_ha + ice_area_ha} has to
account for. The second belongs to the deposition source and governs a
different quantity -- see \code{\link[=build_n_deposition]{build_n_deposition()}}, where WHEP's territory
decides \emph{placement} while HaNi's mask decides the \emph{total}.

Only the first and fourth rows are constants of the inputs;
\code{inst/scripts/diagnose_polycell_support.R} re-derives both. The polygon row
moves with the polity vintage, so read it back off the table in hand rather
than quoting it -- \code{inst/scripts/reconcile_polity_areas.R} measures it.

A fifth land mask is present but deliberately absent from the ladder,
because nothing is measured on it: the GLWD water layer carries the CRU
mask (67,420 cells against LUH2's 64,493 terrestrial), so cells one carries
and the other does not are reported in \code{"water_unmatched"} rather than
dropped by an inner join.
}

\section{What does not vary historically}{

\code{ice_area_ha} comes from \code{ne_10m_glaciated_areas} (see
\code{\link[=read_glaciated_areas]{read_glaciated_areas()}}), a coarse \strong{present-day snapshot}, so it is the
same number in 1850 as in 2015: a historical run carries today's ice
extent, and land that lay under ice in 1850 is credited to
\code{land_area_ha}. This is accepted only because ice is a \strong{reporting
category and not a driver} -- nothing in the package divides by
\code{ice_area_ha} or drives a flux with it. If ice ever becomes a driver, the
source has to be reopened rather than the caveat restated.

Polity geometry is likewise constant within an interval, and the GLWD file
carries a single time step. That is why the default grain is
interval-keyed: no area column varies by year, so a per-year grain would
repeat identical rows about 173 times.
}

\section{The aggregate overlap layer}{

An \strong{aggregate} polity -- \code{BLX-1850-1999} Belgium-Luxembourg,
\code{F249-1918-1990} Yemen, the six residual \code{"Other"} regions -- is a reporting
bucket's territory, and its polygon covers its members'. It therefore cannot
be a row of the partition: two rows claiming the same ground would hand the
cell's land out twice, which is exactly what the rasterised cover this table
replaced did, halving Belgium's 1961 cropland (whep#800).

Dropping it outright is not free either. FAOSTAT keys its pre-2000 data on
those buckets: Belgium (255) and Luxembourg (256) carry no data before 2000,
bucket 15 does, and bucket 15's only territory is \code{BLX-1850-1999}. Measured
against \code{polity_area_crosswalk} over 1850-1961, \strong{ten} of the 460 polities
the pre-1962 resolver reaches are aggregates and \strong{ten} reporting buckets
have no other territory in at least one year: 15, 151, 237 (1954-1961 only),
249 and 901-906.

So both granularities are kept, and they are kept apart. With
\code{aggregates = "overlap_layer"}:
\itemize{
\item every row carries \code{support_role}, \code{"partition"} or \code{"overlap"};
\item \code{"partition"} rows are exactly what \code{"exclude"} emits -- same polities,
same territory, same land, water and ice, split into more intervals only
where an aggregate's validity adds a breakpoint to a cell;
\item the cell's inland water is apportioned over the \strong{partition's} territory
in that cell, so an aggregate receives what its members receive and the
members' share is not diluted by the layer covering them;
\item every diagnostic that describes the partition -- \code{"overlap"},
\code{"unassigned"}, \code{"water_unmatched"}, \code{"footprints"} -- is measured on the
partition alone, so admitting the layer cannot make the polygons look like
they over-claim the validation layer.
}

The consumer contract is the other half: \code{\link[=read_polycell_support]{read_polycell_support()}} returns
the \strong{partition} unless asked otherwise, so no existing consumer can pick
up an overlapping row by accident, and a consumer that wants a bucket's own
territory asks for \code{role = "overlap"} (or \code{"all"}) and states that it is
summing a layer that double-counts by construction. Never aggregate across
the two roles.

\strong{The layer is not a partition of itself either}, and that is not a defect
to be fixed by a tolerance: \code{ROW-1850-2025} Rest of World contains the six
regional residuals it is the sum of. Built on the 19 live aggregates of
\code{whep::polities} (779 rows, ingest 2026-08-13) it is 12,644 polycells, and
at 2015 it puts more territory in a cell than the cell holds in 2,751 cells
shared by \code{ROW} and \code{REUR}, 92 by \code{ROW} and \code{RAFR}, 8 by \code{ROW} and \code{ROCE}
and 7 by \code{ROW} and \code{RLAM} -- plus 2 cells where \code{CODRU-1922-1960} and
\code{EGYSUD-1934-1956} overlap in 1950, which is a polygon disagreement rather
than nesting. So a consumer takes \strong{one polity's} polycells out of the
layer -- the one its bucket resolves to that year -- and never sums the
layer as a whole. That is also why the \code{"overlap"} diagnostic keeps
measuring the partition only: an over-full cell means something there, and
in this layer it means nothing.
}

